WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:07.060
A SIEM receives alerts from many security tools, and each tool rates severity in its own way.

2
00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:13.076
In this demonstration I read a demo alert export, identify the source of each alert,

3
00:00:13.076 --> 00:00:18.949
convert every vendor severity to one common scale, and rank the alerts by urgency.

4
00:00:18.949 --> 00:00:22.888
The alerts are different from the ones in Activity 4.2.

5
00:00:23.987 --> 00:00:29.911
On the Windows workstation, Import-Csv turns each row of the export into an object,

6
00:00:29.911 --> 00:00:37.263
and Select-Object keeps just four columns: the alert ID, the product, the vendor severity and the host.

7
00:00:37.263 --> 00:00:40.331
Eight alerts from seven different products.

8
00:00:41.431 --> 00:00:46.236
First question: which kind of tool raised each alert?

9
00:00:46.236 --> 00:00:48.593
The product name tells us.

10
00:00:48.593 --> 00:00:51.766
Microsoft Defender Antivirus is AV.

11
00:00:51.766 --> 00:00:53.397
Wazuh is our SIEM.

12
00:00:53.397 --> 00:00:57.024
FortiGate is a next-generation firewall.

13
00:00:57.024 --> 00:01:01.919
ModSecurity protects web applications, so it is a WAF.

14
00:01:01.919 --> 00:01:05.273
Symantec DLP is data loss prevention.

15
00:01:05.273 --> 00:01:08.899
Darktrace watches network behavior, NDR.

16
00:01:08.899 --> 00:01:13.160
And CrowdStrike Falcon is EDR, on the endpoint.

17
00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:17.627
Second: look at the severity column.

18
00:01:17.627 --> 00:01:22.569
Severe, 12, High, Critical, Medium seventy-one percent, 7.

19
00:01:22.569 --> 00:01:24.018
Different scales!

20
00:01:24.018 --> 00:01:27.597
Our handout maps them to one common scale.

21
00:01:27.597 --> 00:01:32.113
Wazuh levels 10 to 12 are High and 7 to 9 are Medium.

22
00:01:32.113 --> 00:01:35.351
Darktrace's medium score stays Medium.

23
00:01:35.351 --> 00:01:44.127
Vendor words like Severe and Critical become our High or Critical, but only after we check the details.

24
00:01:45.228 --> 00:01:47.766
Here is why we check the details.

25
00:01:47.766 --> 00:01:52.767
Where-Object picks alert D-01, and Format-List shows every field.

26
00:01:52.767 --> 00:02:01.152
The vendor says Severe, but the file is the EICAR test file, a harmless standard file used to test antivirus,

27
00:02:01.152 --> 00:02:03.537
and it was already quarantined.

28
00:02:03.537 --> 00:02:06.460
After checking, I normalize it to Low.

29
00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:11.075
A tool's severity is a starting point, not the final answer.

30
00:02:12.176 --> 00:02:15.794
You can read the same export on Linux.

31
00:02:15.794 --> 00:02:23.984
cut with a comma delimiter keeps fields 1, 3, 5 and 6: ID, product, severity and host.

32
00:02:25.084 --> 00:02:26.412
Now the ranking.

33
00:02:26.412 --> 00:02:30.646
I rank by what is still happening and by the asset.

34
00:02:30.646 --> 00:02:39.530
D-02 and D-08 are on the same file server and the same account, svc_print: a password spray that succeeded,

35
00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:41.688
then a new scheduled task.

36
00:02:41.688 --> 00:02:47.666
Together that is a compromise in progress, so they are first and second.

37
00:02:47.666 --> 00:02:51.568
D-03 shows a PC trying to reach a known bad IP.

38
00:02:51.568 --> 00:02:55.221
It was blocked, but that PC may be infected.

39
00:02:55.221 --> 00:02:59.788
The blocked attacks and the test file go to the bottom.

40
00:03:00.888 --> 00:03:01.715
Your turn.

41
00:03:01.715 --> 00:03:06.181
In the CTM Virtual Lab, choose Day 4 and Activity 4.2.

42
00:03:06.181 --> 00:03:13.874
Load the ten alerts with Import-Csv, or cut on Linux, and fill in the source, affected asset,

43
00:03:13.874 --> 00:03:18.009
normalized severity and urgency rank for each one.

44
00:03:18.009 --> 00:03:24.047
Use the severity table in your handout, and remember to read the details.

